PHASE 1 — Firestore Rules: nothing new to add
================================================

Good news: none of the Phase 1 Settings fields (notificationsEnabled,
notificationPrefs, isAvailable, locationSharingEnabled, name,
phoneNumber, bloodGroup, address) need a new collection or a new rules
block. They all live directly on the existing users/{userId} document,
which is already covered by the "update" rule you deployed earlier:

    allow update: if isAdmin()
        || (
            isOwner(userId) &&
            request.resource.data.role == resource.data.role &&
            request.resource.data.status == resource.data.status &&
            request.resource.data.rewardPoints == resource.data.rewardPoints
        );

This already lets the owner update any field EXCEPT role, status, and
rewardPoints — which is exactly right for Settings (profile edits,
notification toggles, availability, location-sharing toggle all pass
through fine; a user still can't quietly promote themselves to admin
or change their own reward points).

The `notifications` collection (used by the Cloud Function to write
in-app notification history) is also already covered by your existing
rules:

    match /notifications/{notifId} {
      allow read: if isSignedIn() && resource.data.userId == request.auth.uid;
      allow create: if isSignedIn();
      allow update: if isSignedIn()
          && resource.data.userId == request.auth.uid
          && request.resource.data.diff(resource.data).affectedKeys().hasOnly(['isRead']);
      allow delete: if isAdmin()
          || (isSignedIn() && resource.data.userId == request.auth.uid);
    }

One thing worth double-checking: the Cloud Function in
notificationService.js writes to `notifications` using the Admin SDK,
which always bypasses security rules — so it doesn't matter that a
regular client couldn't technically "allow create: if isSignedIn()"
write on behalf of another user; the Function is trusted server code.

ACTION NEEDED: none. Your current firestore.rules file is already
correct for Phase 1. Just make sure it's deployed:

    firebase deploy --only firestore:rules